The art of flanking in sales is finding the decision people are already trying to make and becoming useful inside it.

A direct mortgage pitch asks, “Are you ready to get preapproved?” A buyer may be asking, “If we buy this place, can one of us still afford to work fewer shifts?” Help them work through that question and you’ve earned a reason to talk financing. Sometimes the answer points to a smaller loan. That’s part of why the approach earns trust.

Six ways to come in from the side

Approach	How it works	When to use it
Solve the decision before the sale	Give buyers a way to compare two homes by total monthly cost, cash needed at closing, and what they’d have left in reserves.	They’re interested but unsure what they can comfortably commit to.
Enter through an existing ritual	Let a run-club organizer opt into a Wild Hawg tasting after a regular meetup. The group already has a reason to gather.	Your product makes sense in a setting where people can experience it firsthand.
Make the proof portable	Give someone a one-page comparison they can text to their spouse, or a sample they can hand to a coworker.	The decision happens in a conversation you won’t be in.
Make the referral useful	Give a realtor a clear way to explain VA assumptions to a buyer, then be available when a specific listing raises questions.	Someone else consistently encounters the problem before you do.
Change the comparison	Show what a product changes about an actual day. For Wild Hawg, the comparison might be carrying a can versus carrying a resealable pouch on a long shift.	The usual comparison leaves out the difference that matters to your buyer.
Let the audience test the claim	Ask testers when they actually reached for the pouch, what they normally use at that moment, and whether they’d buy it again.	You know what makes the product different and need to learn whether anyone values that difference.

The subtle move is to design for the conversation after yours. Picture a nurse on break telling a coworker about Wild Hawg, or a couple at the kitchen table looking at your loan comparison. What could you give them that they’d naturally use in that conversation? A good answer might be a sentence, a sample, or a number they can explain without you.

How I’d use it for Wild Hawg

Test your run-club and nurse-shift lanes with the same product and the same follow-up questions. A run-club tasting gives you an observable first encounter. An opt-in tasting with nurses lets you ask whether the pouch fits a long shift, subject to workplace rules. Track three things in both groups.

1. First use. When did they actually reach for it?
2. Repeat demand. Did they ask where to get another pouch or buy one when they could?
3. Word of mouth. How did they describe it to someone else, in their own words?

That third answer gives you copy you can test. The second tells you whether the copy has a business behind it. If people love the boar and share the video but nobody wants another pouch, you’ve found an attention channel. If people ask for a second pouch and independently name the same moment they’d use it, you have a plausible market entry point.

For the Pantheon, this is semantic surface area applied to a real decision. The same product can enter through taste, portability, ritual, or identity. Each angle attracts a different kind of attention, and you can judge each angle by what people do next. Keep the offer and any paid relationships clear. The indirect part is where you enter the conversation. The buyer should be able to see exactly what they’re choosing.